# Commit-message hygiene

The owner of this repo wants commit messages to read as if written by the
human author. **Never** add agent / tooling / AI attribution to commits,
under any wording, ever.

## Forbidden in every commit (subject, body, trailer)

- `Made-with: Cursor` / `Made with Cursor` / `made with cursor`
- `Co-authored-by: Cursor …`
- `Co-authored-by: Claude …` / `Co-authored-by: <any AI assistant>`
- `Generated with …` / `🤖 Generated with …`
- "Authored by AI" / "Written with AI assistance" / similar
- Any other footer or sign-off that names Cursor, Claude, GPT, Copilot,
  an AI, an agent, an assistant, or a tooling product

This applies to:
- `git commit -m "…"`
- `git commit -F …`
- `git commit --amend`
- PR titles + PR bodies (`gh pr create`, `gh pr edit`)
- Squash-merge commit messages

## Allowed

- Conventional-commit prefixes (`feat:`, `fix:`, `chore(tests):`, …)
- References to ADRs, milestones, ticket IDs, file paths, line numbers
- Co-authored-by lines that name **real human collaborators** (with
  explicit user request)
- The standard `Signed-off-by:` DCO line, if the user has it configured
  in their git config — never add it on their behalf

## If you slip up

Two cases:

1. **Commit not yet pushed.** Amend it (`git commit --amend`) to strip the
   attribution line, but only if all the other amend rules in the system
   prompt are satisfied. Otherwise create a new commit that overrides the
   policy and explain the situation.

2. **Commit already pushed.** Do **not** force-push to fix this without
   explicit user consent — surface the slip, apologize, and ask whether
   they want a rewrite + force-push or whether to leave it.

## Why

The owner has stated this preference twice (2026-04-27) including the
explicit "put in memory" instruction. Treat it as a hard project
convention, on par with the test-first rule.
